m":"DEN","distance":0.659,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Vance Joseph"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Giants went 3-14 in 2024 and were outscored 415 to 273. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 28th in EPA per play and the defense 28th in EPA allowed.","offense":"The Giants offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Daniel Jones ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Malik Nabers, who drew 31% of the targets.","defense":"The Giants had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 28th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their one bright spot was the pass rush, which ranked 3rd in sack rate. Kayvon Thibodeaux was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Brian Burns, who had 8.5 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme was fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature was a Cover 2 rate that ranked 6th lowest in the league.","special":"The Giants were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 50% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 82% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 22% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 50% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Daniel Jones played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,070 yards. Malik Nabers was the clear top target with 31% of the looks and 1,204 receiving yards. Tyrone Tracy handled most of the work in the backfield with 58% of the carries. On defense, Dexter Lawrence led the team with 9 sacks.","coaching":"Brian Daboll called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Jaguars and the 2024 Panthers under Dave Canales. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Panthers under Ejiro Evero and the 2024 Steelers.","trends":"The offense went from 29th in EPA per play in 2023 to 28th in 2024, about where it was. The defense moved from 19th in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 28th in 2024, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was Cover 0 rate, which went from 7% to 3%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}